In 2023, a major airline faced a crisis when an employee accidentally tweeted a sarcastic response to a customer's complaint from the company's official account,
saying "LOL good luck with that" instead of an apology. The tweet went viral within hours, sparking outrage, negative media coverage,
and a 15% stock drop as customers threatened to boycott. The company deleted it and issued apologies, but the damage took weeks to repair, 
costing millions in PR efforts.

WHAT WENT WRONG : No approval process for social media posts; the employee posted impulsively without considering the public platform.

WHAT COULD HAVE BEEN DONE DIFFERENTLY:-
1)Two-person approval rule for all customer-facing posts
2)60-second pause before hitting send
3)Social media policy training emphasizing professional tone
4)Use draft/preview mode to review before publishing

LESSON: One hasty click can destroy brand reputation. Always verify, pause, and get approval for public communications.
